{
  "assessment_summary": "This paper presents a large-scale, pre-registered randomized controlled trial (RCT) comparing cash transfers (~USD 1076 PPP), a WHO-developed psychotherapy program (Problem Management Plus, PM+), and their combination among 5,756 low-income households in rural Kenya. The study finds that cash transfers significantly improve consumption (+20%), assets (+47%), revenue (+26%), and psychological well-being (+0.23 SD) at one year, while PM+ has no detectable effects on any outcome. The combined treatment resembles cash alone. Several important strengths and concerns emerge.\n\n**Strengths:** The study is impressively well-designed and executed. The sample is large (N=5,756), the pre-analysis plan is publicly registered, attrition is remarkably low (4-5%) and non-differential, and baseline balance is achieved across treatment arms. The study employs multiple robustness checks: FDR-adjusted p-values for primary outcomes, a Bayesian negative predictive value analysis for interpreting the PM+ null, an experimenter demand effects test (De Quidt et al. 2018), multiple IPV measurement approaches (envelope task, smiley task, explicit reports), heterogeneity analyses across multiple pre-specified dimensions, CHW fixed effects, and treatment effects over time. The intervention is policy-relevant: PM+ is a WHO flagship program that had previously shown effectiveness in Kenya (Bryant et al. 2017), making the null result informative rather than trivially expected. The comparison with cash transfers—a benchmark intervention increasingly used in development economics—is directly useful for cost-effectiveness calculations and resource allocation decisions.\n\n**Concerns and limitations:** Several issues merit attention. First, the PM+ null result, while carefully documented, raises questions about implementation fidelity at scale that the paper acknowledges but cannot fully resolve. The study scaled from 23 CHWs serving 209 participants (Bryant et al. 2017) to 72 CHWs serving 1,018 participants. While the authors note that the same NGO used the same protocols, the phone verification revealed that only 35% of PM+ and 20% of CT&PM+ recipients recalled receiving all five sessions (vs. 95% per the NGO's records). This discrepancy is substantial and underexplored—it raises questions about session quality, engagement, or recall, and the authors do not investigate which explanation is most plausible or what implications each has for interpreting the null.\n\nSecond, the study population was not selected for psychological distress (only 33% met distress criteria at baseline), which differs from Bryant et al. (2017) where participants were women who had experienced gender-based violence. While the heterogeneity analysis shows PM+ was ineffective even for distressed participants, this subgroup analysis has reduced power and the confidence intervals for the interaction terms are wide enough to include meaningful effects.\n\nThird, the timing issue is important. The endline was ~13 months post-intervention, while Bryant et al. measured at 3 months. The moving-window analysis (Figure F.1) is a creative approach but has limitations: even at the shortest horizon (~200 days/7 months), there are no PM+ effects visible. However, this is still much longer than 3 months, and effects could plausibly have been present at shorter horizons and dissipated between 3-7 months. The authors acknowledge this but somewhat dismiss it as requiring a 'steep decline,' which is actually quite plausible for a brief 5-session intervention.\n\nFourth, the CT&PM+ villages were randomized before baseline and had only 10 households surveyed per village (vs. 30 in other village types). This creates a potential comparability issue, though the baseline balance tests suggest it is not problematic. The consumption effect in the combined treatment (non-significant +7% vs. significant +20% for cash alone) is puzzling and the confidence intervals, while including +18%, also include zero. The authors note this but do not offer a compelling explanation.\n\nFifth, the DFS study overlay adds complexity: 10% of respondents were removed for a separate study on digital financial services, requiring reweighting of non-M-Pesa respondents. While the authors handle this through overweighting, it adds a layer of complexity that could introduce subtle biases.\n\nSixth, the cost-effectiveness analysis is somewhat underdeveloped. The comparison treats non-significant PM+ point estimates as real effects for the cost-effectiveness figure (Figure C.1), which is methodologically problematic—the authors acknowledge this but the exercise is still presented prominently.\n\nSeventh, there is no discussion of potential Hawthorne effects from CHW visits themselves (attention effects), which could either mask or mimic treatment effects. The PM+ spillover households could also have been contaminated if PM+ recipients shared strategies with neighbors.\n\nOverall, this is a high-quality, policy-relevant RCT that addresses an important question at the intersection of development economics and global mental health. The cash transfer results replicate prior work convincingly. The PM+ null is informative but its interpretation requires caution given the differences from the prior successful trial (population, timing, scale). The paper is well-written, transparent about its limitations, and provides extensive supplementary analyses. It represents a significant contribution to the literature on comparative effectiveness of anti-poverty interventions.",
